# TradingView RSI MA Cross

## Source
- Source: [[sources/tradingview-mechanical-strategy-scripts]]
- Page / Timestamp: TradingView script `RSI + MA Strategy`, author handle `The-4xdev-company`, open-source strategy, published 2021-11-23, accessed 2026-05-22, https://www.tradingview.com/script/EZTODAmX-RSI-MA-Strategy/

## Mechanics

### Entry
Use H1 baseline.

- Compute RSI on selected source, default close.
- Compute moving average of RSI with fixed length.
- Long setup:
  - RSI line crosses below its moving average.
  - Reverse-trade input is off.
  - Optional pivot-trading mode is disabled in baseline.
  - No existing position.
- Short setup:
  - RSI line crosses above its moving average.
  - Reverse-trade input is off.
  - No existing position.
- If `Use Reverse Trade` is enabled in P3, swap the long and short conditions as an ablation only.

### Exit
- Exit long on short setup.
- Exit short on long setup.
- V5 safety stop: ATR(14) * 2.0 hard stop because source-visible text says it has position-management functions but does not publish a hard stop formula.

### Stop Loss
- V5 baseline hard stop: ATR(14) * 2.0.
- Optional time stop: 24 H1 bars.

### Position Sizing
V5 default: fixed-risk $1,000 for P2 baseline, one position per symbol/magic.

### Zusatzliche Filter
- RSI upper/lower bounds are visual/reference only in baseline.
- Resolution input can be fixed to chart timeframe or H4 as P3 axis.
- Pivot trading disabled in baseline to keep the first EA deterministic and narrow.

## Concepts (what kind of strategy is this)
- [[concepts/oscillator-crossover]] - RSI crossing its own moving average generates entries.
- [[concepts/mean-reversion]] - source long/short mapping is contrarian around RSI moving average.
- [[concepts/signal-reversal-exit]] - opposite crossover closes the current position.

